WEBVTT

00:00:00.750 --> 00:00:01.295
Welcome!

00:00:01.295 --> 00:00:04.363
In this Java tutorial, you'll copy, move, and

00:00:04.363 --> 00:00:07.363
delete your own practice files, then compare

00:00:07.363 --> 00:00:10.431
file metadata, information such as byte size,

00:00:10.431 --> 00:00:13.294
with content snapshots, text retained from

00:00:13.294 --> 00:00:14.453
successful reads.

00:00:15.098 --> 00:00:17.761
Deliberate file operations and honest

00:00:17.761 --> 00:00:20.927
comparisons help applications preserve saved

00:00:20.927 --> 00:00:23.805
work, manage settings or game state, and

00:00:23.805 --> 00:00:27.044
distinguish a changed observation from an I/O

00:00:27.044 --> 00:00:30.498
failure instead of silently relying on old data.

00:00:31.144 --> 00:00:34.256
Our developer maintains a status file containing

00:00:34.256 --> 00:00:36.072
ready followed by a newline.

00:00:36.072 --> 00:00:39.119
We'll keep an archive copy, rewrite the working

00:00:39.119 --> 00:00:42.037
status as busy with an exclamation mark and a

00:00:42.037 --> 00:00:44.825
newline, and remove only the files this run

00:00:44.825 --> 00:00:45.344
creates.

00:00:45.988 --> 00:00:49.056
The source should remain while its backup gets

00:00:49.056 --> 00:00:50.124
an archive name.

00:00:50.124 --> 00:00:53.125
Both status texts occupy six UTF-8 bytes, yet

00:00:53.125 --> 00:00:54.593
their contents differ.

00:00:54.593 --> 00:00:57.327
Removing the archive once should succeed;

00:00:57.327 --> 00:01:00.462
repeating that request should report that it is

00:01:00.462 --> 00:01:01.463
already absent.

00:01:02.133 --> 00:01:05.125
Now that we're in the Workspace, let's open

00:01:05.125 --> 00:01:07.908
Main.java and build the file comparison.

00:01:11.680 --> 00:01:14.850
We need the Files operations, Path locations,

00:01:14.850 --> 00:01:18.021
UTF-8 encoding, and IOException for failures.

00:01:18.021 --> 00:01:21.332
Main will contain the application, and its main

00:01:21.332 --> 00:01:23.657
method is where execution starts.

00:01:23.657 --> 00:01:27.039
We'll put the file operations inside a try block

00:01:27.039 --> 00:01:28.730
with a matching handler.

00:01:43.804 --> 00:01:46.509
The imports provide short names for those

00:01:46.509 --> 00:01:47.433
library types.

00:01:47.433 --> 00:01:50.600
The class and method enclose the runnable steps;

00:01:50.600 --> 00:01:53.569
the try block lets a file failure skip normal

00:01:53.569 --> 00:01:55.548
reports and reach our handler.

00:01:55.548 --> 00:01:58.385
First, we'll create a separate directory so

00:01:58.385 --> 00:02:01.354
every file we change belongs to this example.

00:02:23.065 --> 00:02:26.248
Creating the temporary directory makes one real

00:02:26.248 --> 00:02:26.925
directory.

00:02:26.925 --> 00:02:30.108
Each resolve call only constructs a child Path;

00:02:30.108 --> 00:02:31.868
it does not create a file.

00:02:31.868 --> 00:02:34.983
The state path is our working original, backup

00:02:34.983 --> 00:02:38.098
is the initial copy location, and archive will

00:02:38.098 --> 00:02:40.265
be the copied file's later name.

00:02:40.285 --> 00:02:43.458
Let's write the developer's initial status, copy

00:02:43.458 --> 00:02:45.243
it, and move only the copy.

00:02:45.243 --> 00:02:47.953
This preserves the original for the later

00:02:47.953 --> 00:02:51.060
rewrite while keeping a separate saved version.

00:03:05.300 --> 00:03:08.318
The write creates ready followed by a newline.

00:03:08.318 --> 00:03:11.139
Copy creates the second file and leaves the

00:03:11.139 --> 00:03:11.598
source.

00:03:11.598 --> 00:03:14.354
Move then changes the copy's location from

00:03:14.354 --> 00:03:15.534
backup to archive.

00:03:15.534 --> 00:03:17.962
It does not move the original source.

00:03:17.962 --> 00:03:20.717
Both destinations began unused in this new

00:03:20.717 --> 00:03:23.669
directory; default copying or moving can fail

00:03:23.669 --> 00:03:25.900
when a destination already exists.

00:03:25.932 --> 00:03:28.175
Now we'll inspect the three paths.

00:03:28.175 --> 00:03:31.277
Existence asks whether an entry is there, while

00:03:31.277 --> 00:03:34.378
the regular-file check asks whether the archive

00:03:34.378 --> 00:03:37.347
holds ordinary file data rather than naming a

00:03:37.347 --> 00:03:38.007
directory.

00:04:00.290 --> 00:04:03.224
After these successful operations, the source

00:04:03.224 --> 00:04:06.223
exists, the old backup path is absent, and the

00:04:06.223 --> 00:04:07.918
archive is a regular file.

00:04:07.918 --> 00:04:10.656
These checks describe observations at that

00:04:10.656 --> 00:04:11.112
moment.

00:04:11.112 --> 00:04:13.655
They cannot guarantee that a later read

00:04:13.655 --> 00:04:16.784
succeeds; another program could change an entry,

00:04:16.784 --> 00:04:19.848
and false can also mean the answer could not be

00:04:19.848 --> 00:04:20.565
determined.

00:04:20.596 --> 00:04:23.487
Locations tell us where the files are, but the

00:04:23.487 --> 00:04:26.127
developer also needs to know what changed.

00:04:26.127 --> 00:04:29.081
We'll retain the source's current text and byte

00:04:29.081 --> 00:04:31.846
size, then rewrite it and read the new text.

00:05:01.370 --> 00:05:04.140
Previous keeps ready and its newline from the

00:05:04.140 --> 00:05:05.494
first successful read.

00:05:05.494 --> 00:05:08.264
PreviousSize stores the byte count as a long,

00:05:08.264 --> 00:05:11.033
the whole-number type returned by Files.size.

00:05:11.033 --> 00:05:13.680
The rewrite changes the working source, and

00:05:13.680 --> 00:05:15.650
current receives the later text.

00:05:15.650 --> 00:05:18.296
The separate archive still holds the copied

00:05:18.296 --> 00:05:20.697
original; rewriting the source does not

00:05:20.697 --> 00:05:22.420
synchronize that other file.

00:05:22.477 --> 00:05:25.713
We'll ask two different questions: does the byte

00:05:25.713 --> 00:05:28.882
size match, and do the read text values differ?

00:05:28.882 --> 00:05:31.377
Each answer needs its own comparison.

00:05:48.348 --> 00:05:51.499
Both texts occupy six bytes here: five ordinary

00:05:51.499 --> 00:05:53.310
characters and one newline.

00:05:53.310 --> 00:05:55.187
Their sizes therefore match.

00:05:55.187 --> 00:05:58.138
Equals compares the String contents, and the

00:05:58.138 --> 00:06:01.155
exclamation operator reverses that Boolean so

00:06:01.155 --> 00:06:02.563
true means different.

00:06:02.563 --> 00:06:05.044
Equal size does not imply equal text.

00:06:05.044 --> 00:06:07.927
These saved Strings are observations, not a

00:06:07.927 --> 00:06:10.073
complete history of every write.

00:06:10.118 --> 00:06:11.748
The comparison is ready.

00:06:11.748 --> 00:06:14.939
Let's remove the archive twice to distinguish a

00:06:14.939 --> 00:06:17.791
successful deletion from an already-absent

00:06:17.791 --> 00:06:20.914
entry, then remove the source before its empty

00:06:20.914 --> 00:06:21.593
directory.

00:06:46.467 --> 00:06:49.181
The first deletion removes the archive and

00:06:49.181 --> 00:06:50.021
returns true.

00:06:50.021 --> 00:06:52.929
The repeated request returns false because no

00:06:52.929 --> 00:06:54.221
entry remains there.

00:06:54.221 --> 00:06:57.258
Other deletion failures still throw exceptions.

00:06:57.258 --> 00:07:00.295
Removing the source leaves the directory empty;

00:07:00.295 --> 00:07:03.138
deleting a nonempty directory this way would

00:07:03.138 --> 00:07:03.462
fail.

00:07:03.462 --> 00:07:05.917
We name only this run's owned entries.

00:07:05.969 --> 00:07:09.052
We'll finish the handler to report the actual

00:07:09.052 --> 00:07:11.381
I/O problem if an operation fails.

00:07:11.381 --> 00:07:14.463
Earlier successful changes may remain, and an

00:07:14.463 --> 00:07:17.203
exception can skip later normal cleanup.

00:07:17.203 --> 00:07:20.423
This small example does not promise to undo the

00:07:20.423 --> 00:07:21.519
entire sequence.

00:07:34.738 --> 00:07:37.605
Our complete program creates its own files,

00:07:37.605 --> 00:07:40.805
preserves a copy under an archive name, compares

00:07:40.805 --> 00:07:43.805
two source observations, and cleans up on the

00:07:43.805 --> 00:07:44.871
successful path.

00:07:44.871 --> 00:07:47.938
Let's click Run and inspect the seven reports.

00:07:53.529 --> 00:07:56.485
The first three reports confirm the retained

00:07:56.485 --> 00:07:59.642
source, absent backup path, and regular archive

00:07:59.642 --> 00:07:59.978
file.

00:07:59.978 --> 00:08:03.067
Same size is true, and content changed is also

00:08:03.067 --> 00:08:05.889
true: matching byte counts hid a real text

00:08:05.889 --> 00:08:06.628
difference.

00:08:06.628 --> 00:08:09.449
The archive deletion reports true and then

00:08:09.449 --> 00:08:09.852
false.

00:08:09.852 --> 00:08:12.740
The following unprinted cleanup removes the

00:08:12.740 --> 00:08:14.554
source and empty directory.

00:08:14.652 --> 00:08:17.398
Apply that reasoning to a different pair of

00:08:17.398 --> 00:08:17.781
texts.

00:08:17.781 --> 00:08:20.399
We'll start with idle and a newline, then

00:08:20.399 --> 00:08:22.314
rewrite playing and a newline.

00:08:22.314 --> 00:08:24.613
Only those two literals will change.

00:08:24.613 --> 00:08:27.422
The paths, copying, moving, comparisons, and

00:08:27.422 --> 00:08:28.827
cleanup stay the same.

00:08:48.288 --> 00:08:50.963
Before running, predict all seven reports.

00:08:50.963 --> 00:08:54.021
Follow the source and copied entry through their

00:08:54.021 --> 00:08:54.658
locations.

00:08:54.658 --> 00:08:57.397
Count the bytes in each text, including its

00:08:57.397 --> 00:09:00.008
newline, then decide the size and content

00:09:00.008 --> 00:09:02.429
comparisons and both deletion results.

00:09:02.429 --> 00:09:04.913
Pause here to work out your prediction.

00:09:13.058 --> 00:09:16.435
Let's click Run and compare your prediction with

00:09:16.435 --> 00:09:17.983
the actual operations.

00:09:23.476 --> 00:09:26.580
Idle and its newline occupy five bytes; playing

00:09:26.580 --> 00:09:28.496
and its newline occupy eight.

00:09:28.496 --> 00:09:31.468
Same size is now false, while content changed

00:09:31.468 --> 00:09:32.195
stays true.

00:09:32.195 --> 00:09:35.299
The location and deletion reports stay the same

00:09:35.299 --> 00:09:37.941
because those operations did not change.

00:09:37.941 --> 00:09:41.045
Running the whole program again creates another

00:09:41.045 --> 00:09:43.885
directory, so each successful run has fresh

00:09:43.885 --> 00:09:44.876
unused targets.

00:09:44.960 --> 00:09:47.919
You used copy and move to preserve one version

00:09:47.919 --> 00:09:50.877
while changing its location, compared metadata

00:09:50.877 --> 00:09:53.772
with actual read text, and deleted only known

00:09:53.772 --> 00:09:54.865
entries in order.

00:09:54.865 --> 00:09:57.567
The reports complete the developer's task.

00:09:57.567 --> 00:10:00.654
In the notebook, the snapshot object repeats the

00:10:00.654 --> 00:10:03.612
read, compare, and update pattern, keeping its

00:10:03.612 --> 00:10:05.735
prior baseline when a read fails.

00:10:05.818 --> 00:10:08.450
For a transfer question, suppose your saved

00:10:08.450 --> 00:10:09.736
observation is ready.

00:10:09.736 --> 00:10:12.307
Another program writes busy and then ready

00:10:12.307 --> 00:10:14.327
before your next successful read.

00:10:14.327 --> 00:10:17.265
What would a content comparison report, and what

00:10:17.265 --> 00:10:19.959
would it leave unknown about the intervening

00:10:19.959 --> 00:10:20.387
writes?

00:10:20.387 --> 00:10:23.203
Explain why a failed read would be a different

00:10:23.203 --> 00:10:23.693
outcome.
